Log management in Linux is a critical part of system administration, security, and troubleshooting.

🔹1. What Is Log Management?

Log management refers to the process of collecting, storing, analyzing, and rotating log files generated by the operating system, applications, and services. It helps with:
	•	Debugging and troubleshooting
	•	Security monitoring (audit trails, intrusion detection)
	•	System performance tuning
	•	Compliance reporting


🔹2. Common Log File Locations

Most logs in Linux are stored under /var/log.

Log File	                   Purpose
/var/log/messages - General system messages (used by many distros)

/var/log/syslog	  - System messages (Debian/Ubuntu)

/var/log/auth.log	   - Authentication logs (login attempts, sudo, etc.)

/var/log/secure	   - Authentication log (RHEL/CentOS)

/var/log/dmesg	   - Kernel ring buffer (hardware-related messages)

/var/log/boot.log	    - Boot process messages
/var/log/cron	Cron job logs

/var/log/httpd/ or /var/log/apache2/	 - Web server logs

/var/log/mysql/	- MySQL/MariaDB logs
/var/log/audit/audit.log	- SELinux/auditd logs


🔹3. Log Management Tools

A. Syslog Systems
	•	rsyslog (default in most distros)
	•	syslog-ng
	•	journald (part of systemd)

🧠 Key Concept:
journald stores logs in binary format, viewable with journalctl.

B. Viewing Logs

# View recent log lines
tail -f /var/log/messages

# Search for keywords
grep "error" /var/log/syslog

# Use journalctl for systemd logs
journalctl -xe          # Show recent errors
journalctl -u sshd      # Logs for SSH service
journalctl --since today


🔹4. Log Rotation

Log rotation ensures logs don’t fill up disk space.

A. Tool: logrotate
	•	Automatically rotates, compresses, and removes old logs.
	•	Config: /etc/logrotate.conf + /etc/logrotate.d/

# Sample logrotate rule
/var/log/httpd/*.log {
    daily
    rotate 7
    compress
    missingok
    notifempty
    create 0640 root utmp
}

	•	Manual trigger:

logrotate -f /etc/logrotate.conf


🔹5. Remote Log Management

Centralized log servers aggregate logs from multiple systems.

Tools:
	•	rsyslog with remote logging
	•	Log aggregation stacks:
	•	ELK Stack: Elasticsearch + Logstash + Kibana
	•	Graylog
	•	Fluentd + Elasticsearch
	•	Splunk (proprietary)

🔹6. Log Security Best Practices
	•	Restrict permissions on /var/log
	•	Monitor logs for unauthorized access
	•	Use auditd for file and system activity auditing
	•	Forward critical logs to a central secure server


🔹7. Troubleshooting with Logs

Example:

# Track failed SSH attempts
grep "Failed password" /var/log/auth.log

# Debug systemd service failures
journalctl -u <service-name>

# Check for hardware errors
dmesg | grep -i error
